Protection from Evil is one of those deceptively simple AD&D spells that actually
has very specific mechanical effects. 
Since your DMG page doesn’t contain the spell text itself, I’ll give you the full 
rules‑accurate breakdown based on the 
Player’s Handbook (PHB) and DMG clarifications.
✨ Protection from Evil — AD&D 1st Edition (PHB)
🎯 Core Effects
Protection from Evil creates a magical defensive aura around the caster (or the touched creature). 
It provides three distinct benefits:
1. +2 bonus to saving throws
Against attacks, spells, or abilities from evil creatures.
2. +2 bonus to AC
Against attacks from evil creatures.
These bonuses apply only to attacks from creatures intending harm and aligned with evil.
🛑 3. Barrier Against Certain Creatures
This is the part many players forget.
The spell creates a magical circle around the caster that prevents bodily contact from:
Summoned creatures
Conjured creatures
Enchanted or magically controlled creatures
Possessing spirits
Mental control attempts (like charm, domination, possession)
These creatures cannot touch the protected character unless:
The protected character attacks them first
The protected character forces contact
The creature uses ranged attacks or magic
This is why Protection from Evil is incredibly strong against demons, devils, 
elementals, invisible stalkers, and magically compelled monsters.
📏 Duration
Cleric version: 3 rounds per level
Magic‑User version: 2 rounds per level
🔄 Reversed Spell: Protection from Good
Works identically but protects against good‑aligned creatures instead.
🧙‍♂️ DMG Clarifications
The DMG adds two important points:
1. The barrier is absolute for summoned creatures.
They cannot cross it unless the protected character breaks the barrier by attacking.
2. The spell does not block missiles or breath weapons.
Only bodily contact is prevented.
🧭 Practical Examples
A demon summoned by a magic‑user cannot touch a protected character.
A vampire under charm control cannot grapple or drain the protected character.
A dominated fighter cannot strike the protected character in melee.
A devil can still throw a fireball or use ranged magic.

