Old Age and Death
When a character dies of old age, no normal resurrection magic can bring them back permanently.
Only magic that extends lifespan (like potions of longevity, wishes, or special artifacts) can delay aging.
Once a character dies from venerable age, it’s final — even resurrection won’t help.
This rule encourages players to think about legacy and family lines as a form of immortality.

🧓 Determining Maximum Age
Every race has a maximum natural lifespan.

When a character reaches old age, roll dice to determine the exact age of death using the 
Maximum Character Age Table:
01–10: Old, lowest age → + d8
11–25: Old, highest age → − d4
26–60: Venerable, lowest age → + d6
61–90: Venerable, highest age → − d10
91–00: Venerable, highest age → + d20
The dice roll modifies the lifespan depending on the race’s age range:
Under 100 years → 1‑year intervals
100–250 years → 10‑year intervals
Over 250 years → 20‑year intervals
Rolls of 0 count as 0 years (not 10 or 20).
In log use ref to DMG15DeathDuetoAge.png


Example:
A dwarf living to “old, lowest age + d8” might reach 251 + (10–80) years.
A half‑orc living to “venerable, highest + d20” might reach 80–99 years.
A high elf living to “venerable, lowest + d6” could reach 1221–1340 years.

