Implement In AD&D 1st Edition, a Scroll of Protection from Magic is one of the most 
powerful protection scrolls.

When read, it creates an invisible anti-magic sphere with a 5-foot radius centered 
on the reader. 
No magic can pass into or out of the sphere, and the sphere moves with the reader.

Effects

Inside the sphere:

Spells cannot affect anything within it.
Magic cannot be cast out of it.
Magical effects crossing the boundary are blocked.
The protection moves with the character who invoked it.
What happens to magic items?

This is the part many groups miss.

According to the AD&D 1e DMG discussion, active magical effects are suppressed, 
and there is also a chance 
that permanent magic items within the area can lose their magic entirely. 
Contemporary analyses of the 1e 
rule note a 50% chance that permanent magic items in the area are rendered nonmagical.

Practical example

If a fighter reads the scroll:

Enemy fireballs, charms, lightning bolts, and similar spells cannot affect 
the fighter while the
protection lasts.
The fighter cannot benefit from friendly buffs either.
A +2 sword entering the sphere may function only as a normal sword while 
inside, or potentially 
lose its enchantment depending on the DM's interpretation of the 1e text.
Physical attacks still work normally. An orc can simply walk up and hit the 
protected character with 
a mundane axe
